From fd30ce59cdc83dd80c6ca8d7763e8b296c65dbef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:58:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: core-update requeue keeps the retry on the
 host of the run's live processes

Card t_01a1dae3ec3f5b57. Incident t_01a1d85b7b4a9e6d (2026-11-14, PR #2946): hermes-sync's
restart-safe requeue reclaimed run 48261 on worker-2 at 09:34:41Z and worker-8 claimed the retry
2 s later. worker-2's `hermes-core-wt ship`, started by the reclaimed run, kept running for at least
104 s: it pushed eba5aef1, placed a land-lane hold and logged review_refused while worker-8's own
ship ran. The two ships pushed three heads in 2m48s.

Five whys
1. Two runs' ships of one card ran at once, on two hosts.
2. The core-update requeue made the card claimable by any pool worker at once, while a process the
   reclaimed run started was still alive on worker-2.
3. The requeue terminated only the worker process. Nothing ended the run's other processes, and for
   persist_on_release jobs (which exist to survive this pause) nothing tied the next claim to their
   host or waited for them to exit.
Root (happened): the requeue released the card without accounting for the run's live processes.
Root (not caught): no test combined a restart-safe requeue, a live run process and a claim from
another host. Same class as t_01a1b77e56b5f51c (orphaned ship plus a second ship).

Fix (new hermes_cli/kanban_requeue_custody.py; only for CORE_UPDATE_REQUEUE_REASON):
- reclaim_task, after the worker is terminated and before the lease is released, lists what the run
  started that is still alive on this host. Ownership reuses existing evidence: processes.json
  entries whose parent_session_id is one of the card's owner_admitted sessions (verified by kernel
  start time), and the run's systemd scope (hermes-worker-kanban-<task>-run-<n>.scope).
- Plain processes (scope pids, non-persisted checkpoint entries) are terminated as a group, TERM
  then KILL, before the release. Persisted jobs and their descendants are spared.
- Live persisted jobs put `custody: {host, run_id, run_uid, processes:[{pid,start,cmd,unit}],
  until}` into the reclaimed payload (same transaction as the status flip, so it syncs with it).
  until = now + max_runtime_seconds (3600 when unset).
- kanban_db_sync.claim_gate (the local seam before every Board acquire) defers another host's claim
  while a custody is open: "requeue_custody: run N's persisted process(es) still live on <host>:
  pid P (<cmd>) ...". The custody host claims as usual.
- The custody host's dispatch tick (sweep) records requeue_custody_released {outcome: exited} once
  every custody pid has exited, or at `until` terminates them (TERM, then KILL) and records
  {outcome: terminated}. A custody whose host never reports stops holding 600 s after `until`.
- No live process at the requeue: no custody key, behaviour unchanged. Other reclaim reasons are
  untouched.
- kanban_worker_scope: _persisted_pids now reads through a shared checkpoint_entries() helper.

Tests (tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_requeue_custody.py, real child processes; only the run scope is
injected). RED with the three hook sites at origin/main and the module present: tests 1, 2, 3, 5,
the spare test and the silent-host test fail (KeyError 'custody' / claim not deferred / processes
alive); test 4 and the other-reason test pass on both sides by design (they pin unchanged
behaviour). GREEN: 8/8.
1. live persisted job: another host's claim deferred naming host, pid, command; same host claims.
2. the job exits: sweep releases (outcome exited); pool claim allowed.
3. bound elapses: TERM-ignoring job killed, released (outcome terminated); pool claim allowed.
4. no live persisted job: no custody; any host claims.
5. plain/foreground command whose child ignores TERM: dead before the release; no custody.

Residual: a replica that applies the status flip and the reclaimed event in different sync pages
could claim in between (both are written in one local transaction); a foreground command on a host
with no systemd user bus has no run scope to find.
